Debian 13 update (2026-10-01)
----------------------------
See QUALIFICATION-DEBIAN13 for the native amd64/arm64 package qualification.
Ubuntu-only scope statements below describe the initial packaging checkpoint.

Debian/Ubuntu packaging for the tar module
==========================================

qore-tar-module contains the native tar module, TarDataProvider source and AOT
modules, compiler metadata, provider logo and locale catalogs, and the qtar
command-line tool and manual page. qore-tar-module-doc contains both HTML API
references and example tests. Debhelper generates matching debug symbols.

Build prerequisites and scope
-----------------------------

Use an isolated native build environment with the packaged Qore runtime and
libqore-dev, then install the Build-Depends from debian/control. Qualification
targets Ubuntu 26.04 amd64 with Qore 3.0 development packages. Other releases
and architectures need their own qualified SDK and native tests. Cross builds
are rejected because Qore tools run during compilation.

The package uses distribution libarchive and requires OpenSSL to keep encryption
available. The minimum Qore runtime and SONAME package versions come from the
SDK used to compile the module; other dependencies come from dh_shlibdeps.
Architecture-specific qmods and shared metadata require the native interpreter;
the runtime package is not marked Multi-Arch: same.

AOT modules and catalogs
------------------------

AOT compilation is enabled explicitly, source-tree qmod symlinks are disabled,
and noopt selects AOT optimization level zero. The Qore SDK propagates the
Debian prefix maps into AOT compilation and metadata generation. Build-time
metadata and tests use the new native module, new qmods and packaged standard
library. The qtar suite inherits this module path for its child processes.

The stripping wrapper copied from Qore preserves appended dependency and
source-location metadata around dh_strip; its real-ELF unit tests run in the
build. dwz skips the runtime package because LLVM DWARF forms are unsupported;
dh_strip still generates matching debug symbols for both modules.

All source-owned catalogs and the project ownership manifest are packaged.
qore-data-provider-i18n checks complete translations for the standard locales
against the built provider. The build therefore requires qore-misc-tools.

Source and binary builds
------------------------

Use a fresh version for each changed upload. This snapshot precedes 1.0.0.
From a clean committed checkout:

    version=1.0.0~git20260928.1
    mkdir -p ../tar-package-work
    git archive --format=tar --prefix="qore-tar-module-$version/" \
        HEAD -- . ':!debian' | xz > \
        "../tar-package-work/qore-tar-module_$version.orig.tar.xz"
    tar -xf "../tar-package-work/qore-tar-module_$version.orig.tar.xz" \
        -C ../tar-package-work
    cp -a debian "../tar-package-work/qore-tar-module-$version/"
    cd "../tar-package-work/qore-tar-module-$version"
    dpkg-buildpackage -S -us -uc

Extract the .dsc with dpkg-source -x into a fresh directory. Install build
dependencies and run unprivileged with networking disabled:

    dpkg-buildpackage -b -us -uc

Architecture-only builds (-B) omit documentation and do not need Doxygen.
Architecture-independent builds (-A) produce documentation. The nodoc build
option suppresses HTML generation; the nodoc profile also omits the doc
package. Do not use nocheck for qualification.

Qualification and publication
-----------------------------

Run all three upstream suites with the freshly built modules. Installed tests
copy only scripts into a temporary directory, explicitly preload the installed
provider qmod and use /usr/bin/qtar. They cover native archives, compression,
encryption, provider actions and CLI behavior without external services.
Tests select C.UTF-8 so Unicode pathname checks execute rather than skip.
The compiler test performs an archive round trip with named arguments and
loads the provider factory in a fresh executable.

Check source/binary Lintian, hardening, matching debug symbols, AOT trailers,
all installed catalogs and resources, a runtime-only install without the SDK,
docs/debug coinstallation, dpkg -V, purge/reinstall, and reproducibility from
two distinct build directories. Retain hashes, dependency inventories and logs.
Testing-PPA publication is separate from approval for a final general release.
